      "text": "Interest in Ayahuasca grew in Western countries during the 1960s, as part of the broader psychedelic movement. Researchers and explorers began to study its effects and cultural significance."
    },
    {
      "date": "1980s",
      "headline": "Ayahuasca Churches Established",
      "text": "Religious movements such as the Santo Daime and União do Vegetal, which incorporate Ayahuasca into their rituals, began to establish churches in Brazil and later spread internationally."
    },
    {
      "date": "2008",
      "headline": "Legal Recognition in Brazil",
      "text": "The Brazilian government officially recognized the religious use of Ayahuasca, allowing its use in spiritual ceremonies by certain religious groups."
    },
    {
      "date": "2010s",
      "headline": "Global Popularity Increases",
      "text": "Ayahuasca gained popularity worldwide as people sought its potential therapeutic benefits, leading to the establishment of Ayahuasca retreats and ceremonies outside of South America."
    },
    {
      "date": "2020",
      "headline": "Research on Therapeutic Uses",
      "text": "Scientific research into the therapeutic uses of Ayahuasca, particularly for mental health conditions such as depression and PTSD, increased significantly."
