      "headline": "First Ironman Triathlon",
      "text": "The first Ironman Triathlon was held in Honolulu, Hawaii. It was conceived by U.S. Navy Commander John Collins and his wife Judy as a challenge to determine which athletes were the fittest: swimmers, cyclists, or runners. The event combined the Waikiki Roughwater Swim, the Around-Oahu Bike Race, and the Honolulu Marathon."
    },
    {
      "date": "1981",
      "headline": "Ironman Moves to Kona",
      "text": "The Ironman Triathlon moved from its original location in Honolulu to the Big Island of Hawaii, with the race now taking place in Kailua-Kona. This location has since become synonymous with the Ironman World Championship."
    },
    {
      "date": "1982",
      "headline": "Ironman World Championship Split",
      "text": "Due to the increasing number of participants, the Ironman World Championship was split into two separate races in February and October. This was the only year the event was held twice."
    },
    {
      "date": "1982-10-09",
      "headline": "Famous Julie Moss Finish",
      "text": "Julie Moss, an amateur athlete, captured the world's attention when she collapsed just yards from the finish line during the Ironman World Championship. Her determination to crawl to the finish line despite her exhaustion became an iconic moment in Ironman history."
    },
    {
      "date": "1990",
      "headline": "Ironman Europe Debut",
      "text": "The first Ironman Europe race was held in Roth, Germany, marking the expansion of Ironman events beyond the United States and Hawaii."
    },
    {
      "date": "1996",
      "headline": "World Triathlon Corporation Formation",
      "text": "The World Triathlon Corporation (WTC) was formed to organize and promote Ironman events worldwide, leading to a more structured and professional approach to the races."
    },
    {
      "date": "2005",
      "headline": "Ironman 70.3 Series Introduction",
      "text": "The Ironman 70.3 series, also known as Half Ironman, was introduced. These races consist of a 1.2-mile swim, a 56-mile bike ride, and a 13.1-mile run, half the distance of a full Ironman."
    },
    {
      "date": "2015",
      "headline": "Ironman Acquired by Wanda Group",
      "text": "The Chinese conglomerate Wanda Group acquired the World Triathlon Corporation, the owner of the Ironman brand, for $650 million, signaling the growing global interest in triathlon events."
    }
  ]
}

