      "text": "Saint James the Greater, one of the Twelve Apostles of Jesus, is martyred in Jerusalem. Tradition holds that his remains were later transferred to Galicia, Spain."
    },
    {
      "date": "0813",
      "headline": "Discovery of Saint James' Tomb",
      "text": "According to legend, the tomb of Saint James is discovered in Galicia by the hermit Pelayo, guided by a star. This leads to the establishment of Santiago de Compostela as a pilgrimage site."
    },
    {
      "date": "0900",
      "headline": "Early Pilgrimages",
      "text": "The first recorded pilgrimages to Santiago de Compostela begin, as the site gains religious significance across Christian Europe."
    },
    {
      "date": "1075",
      "headline": "Construction of Santiago de Compostela Cathedral",
      "text": "Construction of the Santiago de Compostela Cathedral begins, marking a significant development in the pilgrimage route's infrastructure."
    },
    {
      "date": "1122",
      "headline": "Pope Calixtus II Declares Holy Year",
      "text": "Pope Calixtus II declares the first Holy Year for Santiago de Compostela, granting plenary indulgence to pilgrims visiting the shrine during this year."
    },
    {
      "date": "1139",
      "headline": "Codex Calixtinus",
      "text": "The Codex Calixtinus, a guidebook for pilgrims, is compiled. It includes sermons, liturgical texts, and practical advice for pilgrims on the Camino de Santiago."
    },
    {
      "date": "1492",
      "headline": "Decline in Pilgrimage",
      "text": "The discovery of the Americas and the Protestant Reformation contribute to a decline in the number of pilgrims traveling to Santiago de Compostela."
    },
    {
      "date": "1987",
      "headline": "European Cultural Route",
      "text": "The Camino de Santiago is declared the first European Cultural Route by the Council of Europe, recognizing its historical and cultural significance."
    },
    {
      "date": "1993",
      "headline": "UNESCO World Heritage Site",
      "text": "The Camino de Santiago is designated as a UNESCO World Heritage Site, highlighting its importance as a cultural and spiritual journey."
    },
    {
      "date": "2010",
      "headline": "Record Number of Pilgrims",
      "text": "A record number of pilgrims, over 272,000, complete the Camino de Santiago, reflecting its growing popularity in modern times."
    }
  ]
}

