      "headline": "Arrival of Jesuit Missionaries",
      "text": "Jesuit missionaries arrived in Guam in 1668, led by Diego Luis de San Vitores, marking the beginning of Spanish colonization and Christianization of the island."
    },
    {
      "date": "1898",
      "headline": "Ceded to the United States",
      "text": "Following the Spanish-American War, Guam was ceded to the United States under the Treaty of Paris in 1898."
    },
    {
      "date": "1941-12-08",
      "headline": "Japanese Occupation",
      "text": "Guam was invaded and occupied by Japanese forces on December 8, 1941, just hours after the attack on Pearl Harbor."
    },
    {
      "date": "1944-07-21",
      "headline": "Liberation by U.S. Forces",
      "text": "U.S. forces liberated Guam from Japanese occupation on July 21, 1944, during the Battle of Guam in World War II."
    },
    {
      "date": "1950",
      "headline": "Organic Act of Guam",
      "text": "The Organic Act of Guam was enacted in 1950, granting U.S. citizenship to the people of Guam and establishing a civilian government."
    },
    {
      "date": "1962",
      "headline": "End of Naval Administration",
      "text": "In 1962, the U.S. Navy ended its administration of Guam, and the island was placed under the jurisdiction of the Department of the Interior."
    },
    {
      "date": "1972",
      "headline": "Discovery of Shoichi Yokoi",
      "text": "Japanese soldier Shoichi Yokoi was discovered in Guam in 1972, having lived in hiding since World War II, unaware that the war had ended."
